Converted Headers with API Key / request.auth.api_key [Headers with API Key.yml]: the value is kept as the secret variable `headers_with_api_key_api_key`, not in the file
Converted collection [opencollection.yml]: runs use the environment `Environment` by default, since it holds the imported credentials
Converted imported credentials [environments/Environment.yml]: the imported credentials are the secret variables `headers_with_api_key_api_key`; their values are kept in the secret store, not in the file
== Headers with API Key.yml
info:
  name: Headers with API Key
  type: http
  seq: 1

http:
  method: GET
  url: https://api.com/users?X-API-KEY=12345
  params:
    - name: X-API-KEY
      value: "12345"
      type: query
  auth:
    type: apikey
    key: hii
    value: "{{headers_with_api_key_api_key}}"
    placement: header

settings:
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: inherit
  followRedirects: true
  maxRedirects: 5
  forwardAuthorizationHeader: false
== environments/Environment.yml
name: Environment
variables:
  - secret: true
    name: headers_with_api_key_api_key
== opencollection.yml
opencollection: 1.0.0

info:
  name: My Collection

docs:
  content: |-
    ### Welcome to Postman! This is your first collection. 

    Collections are your starting point for building and testing APIs. You can use this one to:

    • Group related requests
    • Test your API in real-world scenarios
    • Document and share your requests

    Update the name and overview whenever you’re ready to make it yours.

    [Learn more about Postman Collections.](https://learning.postman.com/docs/collections/collections-overview/)
  type: text/markdown
bundled: false
extensions:
  bruno:
    ignore:
      - node_modules
      - .git
    presets:
      defaultEnvironment: Environment
